aggressions it took the colombian  government 20 years to recognize part of  the state's responsibility in the case  of jeanette bedoya  the colombian journalist was abducted  tortured and raped while investigating  an armed trafficking network between  paramilitaries and colombian state  officials in 2000  in the name of the columbia state i  recognize our international  responsibility for the failings of the  judicial system  that did not realize a worthy penal  investigation for the victim  and i offer an apology to jeanette  wadoya for the damage it caused  jeanette has campaigned for two decades  to bring her attackers to justice  for her the government's acknowledgement  is another slap in the face  this is a case where part of the  evidence has been lost where my mother  and i have constantly been threatened  with witnesses have been threatened and  killed and where to this day those who  threaten me  say i will be raped again a full apology  requires recognizing that these cases  are not investigated at all  and the states offers no support to  women who suffered the worst possible  violence  was kidnapped in front of police  officers on may 25th  2000 by a group of right-wing  paramilitaries outside a prison in  bogota  and then tortured and raped for 16 hours  before being abandoned  on the side of a road she says prison  guards and the police were involved  and an army general and a police chief  were behind the attack  among others her captors at the time  told her the assault was a direct  message to the press  the case has long lagged in colombia's  justice system  while bedoya has become the symbol of  all women and journalists  fighting against impunity in the country  attacks on journalists and women  continue just like 20 years ago  impunity remains higher than 98 in these  cases  so it's very difficult to believe the  state when it says it's committed in the  fight on crimes against journalists and  women  when clearly it is not the case these  are not failings of a particular moment  in time  they are structural  three former paramilitaries have been  convicted for the attack recently  but none of the masterminds and now the  case reached the inter-american court  for human rights  the court's decision which is definitive  and unappealable  is expected in november and could set a  precedent for  thousands of journalists and victims of  sexual violence  hoping to see justice prevail alessandro  al-jazeera bogota
 

